{"learned":"We have identified two competing causal theories for why outdoor recreation consumers might adopt a differentiated Nalgene bottle. T1 attributes demand to the need for lightweight, durable gear and trust in laboratory‑grade quality. T2 attributes demand to environmental concerns driving preference for reusable containers. Existing public sources provide anecdotal evidence of early outdoor use (scientists taking bottles camping) and rising environmental regulation against disposable cans, but no pre‑1970 data on actual consumer demand, willingness to pay, or retail pathways. The evidence base is insufficient to distinguish the theories or to justify investment based on observed demand. A low‑",
"uncertain":"The core uncertainty is whether outdoor recreation participants in the mid‑1970s would purchase a differentiated Nalgene bottle primarily because of its durability/quality (T1) or because of its environmental benefits (T2). Without direct evidence of purchase behavior or price sensitivity, we cannot determine which driver is stronger. A test that measures real purchase decisions under different value propositions can resolve this.",
"why_next":"A small, affordable market test can generate the first observable data on demand and willingness to pay for differentiated bottles, directly addressing the evidence gap. The €20,000 budget and 12‑week timeline allow a controlled experiment at outdoor retailers, measuring actual sales or pre‑orders rather than stated intentions. This will either provide sufficient evidence to proceed with a differentiated product launch or reveal that demand is insufficient, avoiding a larger investment.","call":{"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Outdoor Recreation vs. Environmental Messaging Test","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Controlled retail placement of three bottle variants (outdoor‑focused, environmental‑focused, generic control) and measurement of actual purchase behavior (sales or pre‑orders).","sample":"200 bottles of each variant (600 total) distributed to 3 outdoor retailers in the Rochester, NY area (e.g., local sporting goods stores, camping shops). Retailers will display each variant on separate shelves with identical pricing (proposed €8 per bottle) and minimal branding beyond the value proposition label. Sales will be tracked daily for 10 weeks; any unsold inventory will be offered for pre‑order with a deposit of €2 to gauge willingness to commit.","measure":"Number of units sold or pre‑ordered for each variant during the 10‑week period. Secondary measure: average deposit amount for pre‑orders (capped at €2) to assess willingness to pay beyond price.","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"The outdoor‑focused variant will generate at least 30 sales/pre‑orders, significantly exceeding the generic control (≤10). The environmental variant will perform similar to generic (≤12).","T2":"The environmental‑focused variant will generate at least 30 sales/pre‑orders, significantly exceeding generic (≤10). The outdoor variant will perform similar to generic (≤12)."},"decision_rule":"If either differentiated variant achieves ≥30 sales/pre‑orders and its sales are ≥3× the generic control, proceed to a pilot production run of 5,000 bottles for the winning value proposition. If neither differentiated variant meets the threshold, stop further consumer product development and consider licensing or no expansion. If both variants exceed the threshold, conduct a second test with larger sample to compare magnitude of demand.","cost_eur":20000,"weeks":12}}}

{"learned": "From public sources before early 2009, Aspen Aerogels is known for high\u2011performance flexible aerogel blankets used in industrial, military, and aerospace applications. A 2005 company claim describes the blanket format as suitable for energy conservation in homes, but does not show consumer demand. A 2007 independent review of aerogel\u2011insulated footwear highlights superior thermal performance and NASA origin, supporting the premium performance narrative (T1). A 2001 NASA spinoff article notes that falling prices could broaden consumer use, hinting at price sensitivity. No evidence directly shows residential homeowners\u2019 willingness to purchase Aspen\u2019s aerogel blanket, nor their preference for\u2011",
"uncertain": "whether homeowners will value the product as a premium performance item (T1), an eco\u2011friendly choice (T2), or are primarily driven by price. The lack of consumer survey data means demand is unknown. The two existing theories predict different price\u2011point responses, but we have not tested actual purchase intent.",
"why_next": "A low\u2011cost test offering real pre\u2011order deposits can distinguish the two value propositions by measuring purchase intent at different price points and messaging. It fits the \u20ac20,000 budget and 12\u2011week timeline, and would provide concrete data to decide whether to proceed with a differentiated consumer proposition or abandon the idea.","call": {"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Premium vs Eco\u2011positioned aerogel blanket pre\u2011order test","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Online recruitment of homeowners, random assignment to two arms. Each arm views a product page with identical specifications (flexible aerogel blanket, size suitable for a typical bedroom wall). Arm A is framed as \"high\u2011performance, NASA\u2011developed insulation\" priced at 20% above a conventional fiberglass blanket. Arm B is framed as \"eco\u2011friendly, carbon\u2011reducing insulation\" priced at 15% above a conventional blanket. Participants are offered a refundable \u20ac10 deposit to secure a pre\u2011order for a single blanket. The deposit is returned if the test ends without purchase. Measure: number of deposits placed in each arm (conversion rate).","sample":"200 homeowners recruited via an online panel targeting homeowners aged 30\u201165, with a mix of income and geography. Stratify to ensure at least 50 participants with self\u2011reported high environmental concern.","measure":"Conversion rate = deposits / participants per arm. Also collect self\u2011reported environmental concern (high/medium/low).","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"Arm A conversion \u2265 15% and exceeds Arm B by at least 3 percentage points, indicating premium performance justification.","T2":"Arm B conversion \u2265 12% and exceeds Arm A by at least 3 percentage points, indicating eco\u2011positioning justification."},"decision_rule":"If Arm A meets its prediction and outperforms Arm B, recommend developing a premium, high\u2011performance consumer proposition. If Arm B meets its prediction and outperforms Arm A, recommend an eco\u2011focused proposition. If neither arm meets its target or the difference is <3pp, conclude insufficient evidence to justify investment and stop. Proceed to a pilot production run of 500 units for the winning proposition within the next 6 months, otherwise cease consumer expansion.","cost_eur":5000,"weeks":8}}}

{"learned":"We have read several sources showing Hilti\u2019s limited consumer reach, strong price sensitivity among DIY buyers, and that 70% of power tool purchasers are homeowners. Evidence indicates consumers prefer cheaper imports unless professional reliability is communicated. No direct data on Hilti\u2019s consumer willingness to pay premium exists. Two open theories explain potential demand: T1 (premium acceptance driven by professional reputation) and T2 (premium acceptance but price remains primary driver). A third theory could posit that hobbyists seek specialized features not offered by professionals, suggesting a different product focus. The test budget (\u20ac20k) and 12\u2011week limit allow a modest online/",
"uncertain":"Whether homeowners will pay a premium for Hilti\u2019s professional reputation, and how price sensitivity interacts with brand perception. Also unclear is the size of the addressable DIY market that Hilti could capture without diluting its professional brand.",
"why_next":"We need a test that can distinguish between the two existing theories (and potentially a third) by measuring actual purchase intent under different price points while keeping product attributes constant. A survey\u2011based simulated purchase can be run within budget and time, providing quantitative data on willingness to pay. If the test shows significant purchase intent at a premium price, we can justify a pilot; if not, we should leave the market alone.","call":{"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Premium Price Willingness Survey","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Online survey with simulated purchase choice and price variation","sample":"500 homeowners/hobbyists recruited via panel (target 50% DIY active)","measure":"Choice between Hilti professional\u2011grade tool at \u20ac199 (premium) vs generic low\u2011cost tool at \u20ac99. Secondary measure: stated willingness to pay extra for durability (0\u20ac100 scale).","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"At least 30% will choose Hilti despite the higher price, and durability will rank as top driver.","T2":"Choice for Hilti will be price\u2011sensitive: at most 15% will choose Hilti at \u20ac199, and price will be the primary stated driver."},"decision_rule":"If \u2265 20% of respondents choose Hilti at \u20ac199, proceed to a small pilot (e.g., 100 units sold via Hilti\u2019s direct\u2011to\u2011consumer channel). If \u2264 15% choose Hilti, stop expansion.","cost_eur":15000,"weeks":10}}}

{"learned":"The dossier shows Koppert already serves home gardeners through its Canadian subsidiary and has provided biological controls to individual growers (e.g., Martha Stewart’s farm). However, there is no quantitative evidence on home gardener demand, willingness to pay, or adoption rates. The French regulatory ban on phytosanitary sales to amateurs suggests a potential barrier, but biological controls may be exempt or face similar restrictions. Two open theories explain why home gardeners might buy: T1 (convenience/safety) and T2 (status/signaling). Evidence so far only confirms Koppert’s capability to sell to this segment, not the underlying motivation. With no searches left, we must rely on the",
"uncertain":"Whether home gardeners will pay a premium for Koppert’s products and which value proposition (convenience/safety vs. status) drives purchase intent.",
"why_next":"A low‑cost test can separate the theories by offering two distinct retail packages (convenience‑focused vs. status‑focused) against a control, measuring purchase intent or actual pre‑orders. The €20k budget and 12‑week window allow a focused online or retail pilot. If the test shows a statistically significant uplift for one package over the other and above a pre‑defined threshold (e.g., ≥15% higher intent), we can conclude which driver is more promising and justify further expansion. If neither package outperforms the control, the evidence suggests limited demand for a differentiated home‑gardener proposition, supporting staying with professional growers.","call":{"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Home Gardener Retail Package Test","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Online survey with simulated purchase scenario and optional pre‑order (deposit) for lady beetles.","sample":"300 home gardeners recruited via gardening forums and social media, balanced for age and garden size.","measure":"Purchase intent (1‑10 scale) and actual deposit conversion rate for each package variant.","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"Convenience‑branded package will achieve ≥20% higher intent and deposit rate than control; status package will not differ from control.","T2":"Status‑branded package will achieve ≥15% higher intent and deposit rate than control; convenience package will not differ from control."},"decision_rule":"If either package shows a statistically significant uplift (p<0.05) above its respective threshold compared to control, proceed to a pilot retail launch for that package. If neither package meets its threshold, recommend staying focused on professional growers. If both meet thresholds, prioritize the package with higher uplift for pilot.","cost_eur":15000,"weeks":10}}}

{"learned":"We have identified that by late 1974, Statitrol has ionization smoke‑detection technology, a 1974 Sears retail partnership, and the new NFPA 74 and UL 217 standards that enable battery‑operated residential detectors. Evidence shows industrial use of ionization detectors since the 1940s and that HUD installed them in 17,000 mobile homes, indicating some market acceptance. However, there is no direct evidence that homeowners will purchase Statitrol’s specific consumer model or pay a premium for it.

Two open theories explain adoption:
• T1: Homeowners adopt because they trust industrial reliability and will pay a premium for a self‑monitoring, battery‑powered solution that meets the new codes.","uncertain":"T2 agrees on the role of battery convenience but claims convenience, not reliability, is the primary driver. Both predict at least 15% higher purchase intent versus a comparable battery‑only detector, making the theories indistinguishable without isolating the driver (reliability vs convenience) and the willingness to pay a premium.","why_next":"A low‑cost test can separate the mechanisms by comparing a self‑monitoring version (premium price) to a battery‑only version (standard price) sold through Sears. T1 expects the self‑monitoring version to outsell the battery‑only version by ≥15%; T2 expects no significant difference because convenience, not the self‑monitoring feature, drives purchase. The result will clarify whether a premium, reliability‑focused proposition is justified and whether Statitrol should pursue a differentiated consumer product now.","call":{"name":"propose_test","arguments":{"name":"Sears test of self‑monitoring vs battery‑only detectors","theories":["T1","T2"],"method":"Deploy two versions of Statitrol’s ionization smoke detector through a subset of Sears stores for 12 weeks: (A) Self‑monitoring model with battery backup, priced at €30; (B) Battery‑only model without self‑monitoring, priced at €25. Both meet UL 217 and NFPA 74 standards. Track units sold and collect brief post‑purchase surveys (or in‑store QR‑code questionnaire) asking buyers to rank purchase drivers (reliability/industrial trust, convenience/ease of installation, price, self‑monitoring feature).","sample":"Estimated 200 stores in the Denver metro area (where Statitrol is based) with historical smoke‑alarm sales data. Allocate 100 stores to each model. Expected baseline sales of ~5 units per store per month for comparable battery detectors (based on 1974 Sears data for First Alert).","measure":"Primary: sales volume of Model A vs Model B over 12 weeks. Secondary: survey responses ranking drivers; willingness to pay extra for self‑monitoring (asked of Model A buyers whether they would have bought Model B at €25).","prediction_by_theory":{"T1":"Model A sales ≥15% higher than Model B; ≥40% of Model A buyers cite reliability/self‑monitoring as primary driver; ≥60% indicate they would not have bought Model B at the lower price.","T2":"Model A sales ≈ Model B (difference <5%); purchase drivers dominated by convenience and price; ≤20% cite reliability/self‑monitoring as primary; willingness to pay extra for self‑monitoring ≤30%."},"decision_rule":"If Model A sales exceed Model B by ≥15% AND ≥40% of buyers cite reliability/self‑monitoring as primary driver, recommend proceeding with a differentiated consumer line emphasizing self‑monitoring and premium pricing (support T1). If Model A sales are ≤5% higher than Model B and convenience/price dominate survey responses, recommend focusing on a standard battery‑only offering without premium pricing (support T2). Intermediate results (5‑15% difference) lead to a follow‑up focus group to refine positioning.","cost_eur":18000,"weeks":12}}}

{"learned": "From public sources before mid\u20111991 we learned: Diatek had an infrared ear thermometer (Model 7000) for hospitals, derived from NASA technology. A rival patent shows non\u2011contact thermometers existed. A 1990 study shows 57.9% of parents owned mercury thermometers but only 9.7% could read them accurately, indicating a pain point. No source shows Diatek already sold to consumers or any consumer demand data. Two open theories explain why parents might adopt a consumer version. T1 argues adoption would be driven by desire for accuracy and safety (avoiding mercury). T2 argues adoption would be driven by desire for convenience and speed (quick measurements). Both theories predict purchase intent at",
 "uncertain": "least 20% higher than a generic digital thermometer, but they attribute the increase to different motivations. There is no direct evidence that either motivation is the primary driver, nor evidence of willingness to pay, competitive landscape, or regulatory feasibility for a specific use case such as home temperature monitoring for children or pets.",
 "why_next": "A test can separate the theories by measuring which motivation actually influences purchase behavior. The test must be affordable (\u20ac20,000) and fit a 12\u2011week timeline. By offering the same physical thermometer with different value propositions (accuracy/safety vs. convenience/speed) and measuring actual purchase decisions, we can observe whether the decision to buy correlates with the emphasized attribute. This would provide the first empirical evidence on consumer demand and the primary driver, which is needed to justify investment. If the test shows a significant difference in purchase rates between the two value propositions, we can infer which theory better explains consumer behavior and",
 "call": {"name": "propose_test", "arguments": {"name": "Consumer Messaging Test: Accuracy/Safety vs. Convenience/Speed", "theories": ["T1", "T2"], "method": "Online controlled experiment with a mock e\u2011commerce landing page offering the Diatek infrared ear thermometer (identical product description) but with two different value proposition banners. Group A sees \"Medical\u2011grade, mercury\u2011free, proven accurate \u2013 safe for your child.\" Group B sees \"Measure in 2 seconds, no contact \u2013 fast and convenient for busy parents.\" Both groups see identical price (\u20ac29.99, based on 1991 consumer electronics price points) and purchase button. Participants are recruited from a panel of U.S. parents with children under 12 (demographics matching the 1990 study). After viewing the page, they are asked to either add to cart or skip. Those who add to cart are offered a pre\u2011order with a 50% deposit (refundable if they change mind) to simulate commitment.", "sample": "300 parents (150 per group), recruited via a panel that mimics 1991 U.S. parent demographics. Recruitment cost estimated \u20ac4,000. Platform and mock site cost \u20ac3,000. Deposit processing and fulfillment simulation cost \u20ac2,000. Total estimated \u20ac9,000, well under \u20ac20,000.", "measure": "Primary outcome: conversion rate (add to cart) per group. Secondary outcome: deposit acceptance rate (pre\u2011order completion). Also collect a short survey question asking why they chose to purchase (accuracy/safety vs. convenience/speed).", "prediction_by_theory": {"T1": "Group A (accuracy/safety banner) will have a conversion rate at least 10 percentage points higher than Group B.", "T2": "Group B (convenience/speed banner) will have a conversion rate at least 10 percentage points higher than Group A."}, "decision_rule": "If the difference in conversion rates between groups is >=10 percentage points (e.g., 25% vs 15%), the test is conclusive: the group with the higher rate supports the corresponding theory. If the difference is <10 percentage points, the test is inconclusive and we recommend further qualitative research (e.g., focus groups) before deciding. The 10\u2011point threshold is chosen as a reasonable effect size given sample size and budget constraints.", "cost_eur": 9000, "weeks": 10}}
}
